    35	  - keep one black n x n mover inside a sparse blue channel system,
    36	  - the channel geometry is now a straight main ray plus one or two perpendicular side branches,
    37	  - the black square does not BFS through turns; it continues only along the unique longest straight
    38	    ray that starts at its current placement,
    39	  - it stops when that ray reaches the grid edge.
    40	- Small trace:
    41	  - Example: n=2 on a 14 x 14 grid. The mover sits at top-left (5,2). The main blue strip runs
    42	    horizontally to the right edge, with only a one-step blue stub behind the mover. Farther right,
    43	    at column 8, a vertical branch goes upward to the top edge.
    44	  - Old corridor-following logic with edge-stop would BFS through the turn and could prefer the
    45	    upward edge because it is farther in path length.
    46	  - The fused rule instead compares the straight rays leaving the mover: left has length 1, right
    47	    has length 10, vertical directions have length 0 at the mover. Therefore the mover slides only
    48	    right and finishes flush against the right border.
    49	
    50	b) Complexity calibration
    51	-------------------------
    52	- If the grid contained only one isolated straight line, the task would become too close to a toy
    53	  case and the straight-ray constraint would not be visibly necessary.
    54	- The side branches matter because they create legal traversable turns that a BFS-based edge-stop
    55	  solver would use, but the fused rule forbids turning.
    56	- To keep the rule learnable, the generator restricts variation to a small set:
    57	  - orientation: horizontal or vertical,
    58	  - motion direction: one of the four cardinal directions,
    59	  - short opposite-side stub length: 0 to 2,
    60	  - one required edge-reaching side branch and an optional short second branch.
    61	- This is enough variation for ARC-style pattern induction without creating too many cases.
    62	
    63	c) Hidden-assumption audit of the original main()
    64	-------------------------------------------------
    65	- Original assumption: there are at least two same-sized solid squares.
    66	  - Broken by D5/2. The fused solver must detect a single solid mover square.
    67	- Original assumption: the river plus mover plus destination define traversability.
    68	  - Broken by D5/2. Traversability is now just river union mover.
    69	- Original assumption: BFS over 4-neighbor placement moves is the correct motion model.
    70	  - Broken by D1/0. In the fused task, turning into a side branch is illegal even when feasible.
    71	- Original assumption: the chosen goal is either the red square or the farthest BFS-reachable
    72	  placement.
    73	  - Broken by D1/0 and D5/2 together. The goal is the end of the longest straight ray from the
    74	    mover, and the generator guarantees that this ray ends on the boundary.
    75	- New solver assumptions introduced here:
    76	  - exactly one solid non-background square component exists,
    77	  - feasible motion from the mover is axis-aligned,
    78	  - among the four cardinal directions there is a unique longest straight feasible ray,
    79	  - that longest ray reaches the boundary,
    80	  - side branches never touch the mover directly, so they do not create local ambiguity.
    81	  - The generator explicitly enforces these constraints.
    82	
    83	d) Placement legality
    84	---------------------
    85	- All geometry is built in placement space first and then painted as n-pixel-thick strips.
    86	- The main strip always keeps the mover fully in bounds and always reaches exactly one intended
    87	  stopping edge without using a corner.
    88	- The backward stub is short and stays interior, so the opposite direction is visible but inferior.
    89	- Branches attach only at interior points of the main strip, never at the mover, never at corners,
    90	  and never overlap each other except at their single junction cells with the main strip.
    91	- The occupied non-background area is constrained to 10-20% of the grid, matching the sparse-narrow
    92	  requirement.
    93	
    94	e) Visual observability
    95	-----------------------
    96	- Humans can see one black square, no red target, a dominant green background, and thin blue
    97	  rectilinear channels.
    98	- The output repeatedly shows the black square continuing straight to a border instead of turning
    99	  into visibly available side branches.
   100	- The direction cue is geometric rather than based on color ids: the mover sits on a straight line
   101	  with a very short stub on one side and a much longer continuation on the other.
   102	
   103	f) Visual cleanliness
   104	---------------------
   105	- Only three colors remain: green background, blue channel, black mover.
   106	- All blue shapes are thin axis-aligned strips with low density; the grid stays mostly background.
   107	- Branch count is limited to at most two, which keeps the scene sparse and legible.
   108	
   109	g) Color-change inferability
   110	----------------------------
   111	- There is no hidden color remapping. The only output change is that the black square vacates its
   112	  start location back to blue and reappears at the ray endpoint on the border.
   113	
   114	h) Anti-degeneration target
   115	---------------------------
   116	- To prove the fusion is not just "edge-stop on any corridor", every generated sample includes a
   117	  required branch that makes unrestricted BFS edge-stop disagree with the fused straight-ray rule.
   118	- Concretely, the required branch reaches a border through a longer path than the straight main ray,
   119	  so a BFS edge-stop baseline would turn into that branch, while the fused solver must stay straight.
   120	
   121	i) Generator obligations for the new solver
   122	-------------------------------------------
   123	- The generator must guarantee:
   124	  - exactly one salient black solid square,
   125	  - sparse blue coverage ratio in [10%, 20%],
   126	  - a unique longest straight ray from the mover,
   127	  - that longest ray ends on a border but not a corner,
   128	  - at least one side branch exists and creates a different BFS-edge-stop answer,
   129	  - oracle check: main(input) matches the analytically constructed expected output.
   130	"""
   131	
   132	
   133	_BG, _RIVER, _MOVER = 3, 1, 0
   134	_DIRS = ((0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0))
   135	
   136	
   137	def _detect_mover(g: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> dict:
   138	    squares: list[dict] = []
   139	    for comp in find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=True, return_format="dict"):
   140	        col = int(comp["color"])
   141	        b = comp["bbox"]
   142	        if bbox_h(b) != bbox_w(b):
   143	            continue
   144	        patch = g[int(b[0]) : int(b[2]) + 1, int(b[1]) : int(b[3]) + 1]
   145	        if not bool((patch == col).all()):
   146	            continue
   147	        squares.append({"col": col, "r0": int(b[0]), "c0": int(b[1]), "n": int(bbox_h(b))})
   148	    assert squares
   149	    return max(squares, key=lambda s: (int(s["n"]), -int(s["col"])))
   150	
   151	
   152	def _find_river(g: np.ndarray, bg: int, mover_col: int) -> int:
   153	    counts = Counter(map(int, g.flatten()))
   154	    for col, _ct in counts.most_common():
   155	        if int(col) in (int(bg), int(mover_col)):
   156	            continue
   157	        return int(col)
   158	    raise AssertionError("river color not found")
   159	
   160	
   161	def _feasible_windows(trav: np.ndarray, n: int) -> np.ndarray:
   162	    H, W = trav.shape
   163	    h2 = int(H - n + 1)
   164	    w2 = int(W - n + 1)
   165	    assert not (h2 <= 0 or w2 <= 0)
   166	    feasible = np.zeros((h2, w2), dtype=bool)
   167	    for r in range(h2):
   168	        for c in range(w2):
   169	            if bool(trav[int(r) : int(r + n), int(c) : int(c + n)].all()):
   170	                feasible[int(r), int(c)] = True
   171	    return feasible
   172	
   173	
   174	def _touches_boundary(r: int, c: int, n: int, H: int, W: int) -> bool:
   175	    return int(r) == 0 or int(c) == 0 or int(r + n) == H or int(c + n) == W
   176	
   177	
   178	def _trace_ray(feasible: np.ndarray, sr: int, sc: int, dr: int, dc: int) -> tuple[int, int, int]:
   179	    h2, w2 = feasible.shape
   180	    steps = 0
   181	    r, c = int(sr), int(sc)
   182	    while True:
   183	        nr = int(r + dr)
   184	        nc = int(c + dc)
   185	        if not (0 <= nr < h2 and 0 <= nc < w2 and bool(feasible[nr, nc])):
   186	            return steps, int(r), int(c)
   187	        steps += 1
   188	        r, c = int(nr), int(nc)
   189	
   190	
   191	def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
   192	    """
   193	    Combo rule:
   194	    - detect the single solid mover square,
   195	    - compute all feasible n x n placements inside the sparse channel,
   196	    - compare the four straight rays leaving the mover,
   197	    - choose the unique longest straight ray and slide to its endpoint,
   198	    - repaint the start square back to river.
   199	    """
   200	    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)
   201	    H, W = g.shape
   202	    bg = int(Counter([int(g[0, 0]), int(g[0, -1]), int(g[-1, 0]), int(g[-1, -1])]).most_common(1)[0][0])
   203	
   204	    mover = _detect_mover(g, bg)
   205	    river = _find_river(g, bg, int(mover["col"]))
   206	
   207	    n = int(mover["n"])
   208	    sr, sc = int(mover["r0"]), int(mover["c0"])
   209	    trav = (g == int(river)) | (g == int(mover["col"]))
   210	    feasible = _feasible_windows(trav, n)
   211	    assert bool(feasible[int(sr), int(sc)])
   212	
   213	    rays = []
   214	    for dr, dc in _DIRS:
   215	        steps, er, ec = _trace_ray(feasible, sr, sc, dr, dc)
   216	        rays.append(
   217	            {
   218	                "steps": int(steps),
   219	                "end_r": int(er),
   220	                "end_c": int(ec),
   221	                "edge": int(_touches_boundary(er, ec, n, H, W)),
   222	            }
   223	        )
   224	
   225	    best = max(rays, key=lambda item: (int(item["steps"]), int(item["edge"])))
   226	    tr, tc = int(best["end_r"]), int(best["end_c"])
   227	
   228	    out = g.copy()
   229	    out[int(sr) : int(sr + n), int(sc) : int(sc + n)] = int(river)
   230	    out[int(tr) : int(tr + n), int(tc) : int(tc + n)] = int(mover["col"])
   231	    return out
   232	
   233	
   234	def _size_to_grid(n: int) -> int:
   235	    if n == 1:
   236	        return random.randint(10, 13)
   237	    if n == 2:
   238	        return random.randint(12, 16)
   239	    if n == 3:
   240	        return random.randint(15, 18)
   241	    return random.randint(19, 20)
   242	
   243	
   244	def _paint_main_strip(grid: np.ndarray, orient: str, fixed: int, a0: int, a1: int, n: int) -> None:
   245	    lo, hi = sorted((int(a0), int(a1)))
   246	    if orient == "h":
   247	        grid[int(fixed) : int(fixed + n), int(lo) : int(hi + n)] = _RIVER
   248	    else:
   249	        grid[int(lo) : int(hi + n), int(fixed) : int(fixed + n)] = _RIVER
   250	
   251	
   252	def _paint_branch_to_edge(grid: np.ndarray, orient: str, fixed: int, anchor: int, side: int, n: int) -> int:
   253	    G = int(grid.shape[0])
   254	    if orient == "h":
   255	        if side < 0:
   256	            grid[0 : int(fixed + n), int(anchor) : int(anchor + n)] = _RIVER
   257	            return int(fixed)
   258	        grid[int(fixed) : G, int(anchor) : int(anchor + n)] = _RIVER
   259	        return int(G - n - fixed)
   260	    if side < 0:
   261	        grid[int(anchor) : int(anchor + n), 0 : int(fixed + n)] = _RIVER
   262	        return int(fixed)
   263	    grid[int(anchor) : int(anchor + n), int(fixed) : G] = _RIVER
   264	    return int(G - n - fixed)
   265	
   266	
   267	def _paint_partial_branch(grid: np.ndarray, orient: str, fixed: int, anchor: int, side: int, length: int, n: int) -> None:
   268	    length = int(length)
   269	    if orient == "h":
   270	        if side < 0:
   271	            grid[int(fixed - length) : int(fixed + n), int(anchor) : int(anchor + n)] = _RIVER
   272	        else:
   273	            grid[int(fixed) : int(fixed + n + length), int(anchor) : int(anchor + n)] = _RIVER
   274	    else:
   275	        if side < 0:
   276	            grid[int(anchor) : int(anchor + n), int(fixed - length) : int(fixed + n)] = _RIVER
   277	        else:
   278	            grid[int(anchor) : int(anchor + n), int(fixed) : int(fixed + n + length)] = _RIVER
   279	
   280	
   281	def _farthest_edge_stop(grid: np.ndarray, mover_r: int, mover_c: int, n: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
   282	    trav = (grid == _RIVER) | (grid == _MOVER)
   283	    feasible = _feasible_windows(trav, n)
   284	    h2, w2 = feasible.shape
   285	    dist = -np.ones((h2, w2), dtype=int)
   286	    dist[int(mover_r), int(mover_c)] = 0
   287	    q = [(int(mover_r), int(mover_c))]
   288	    head = 0
   289	    while head < len(q):
   290	        r, c = q[head]
   291	        head += 1
   292	        for dr, dc in _DIRS:
   293	            rr = int(r + dr)
   294	            cc = int(c + dc)
   295	            if 0 <= rr < h2 and 0 <= cc < w2 and bool(feasible[rr, cc]) and int(dist[rr, cc]) == -1:
   296	                dist[rr, cc] = int(dist[r, c] + 1)
   297	                q.append((int(rr), int(cc)))
   298	
   299	    edge_positions: list[tuple[int, int]] = []
   300	    for r in range(h2):
   301	        for c in range(w2):
   302	            if int(dist[r, c]) < 0:
   303	                continue
   304	            if _touches_boundary(r, c, n, grid.shape[0], grid.shape[1]):
   305	                edge_positions.append((int(r), int(c)))
   306	    assert edge_positions
   307	    return max(edge_positions, key=lambda pos: (int(dist[pos[0], pos[1]]), -abs(pos[0] - mover_r) - abs(pos[1] - mover_c)))
   308	
   309	
   310	def _occupancy_ratio(grid: np.ndarray) -> float:
   311	    return float((grid != _BG).sum()) / float(grid.shape[0] * grid.shape[1])
   312	
   313	
   314	def _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:
   315	    n = random.choice([1, 2, 3, 4])
   316	    G = _size_to_grid(n)
   317	    H = W = G
   318	    orient = random.choice(["h", "v"])
   319	    sign = random.choice([-1, 1])
   320	    grid = np.full((H, W), _BG, dtype=int)

